<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Onogurs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Onogurs were a Turkic-speaking nomadic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, first recorded in 463 CE when they sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Their name is usually interpreted as 'ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghurs">Oghurs</a>', meaning ten tribes. In the 7th century they formed, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>, and their name is thought to survive in the word 'Hungarian'.</p></div>
